  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nUnsettled weather will continue into the early part of the week \nahead, but it is widespread beneficial rainfall is looking less \nlikely. A split trough approaches the area on Monday, with the \nnorthern split passing to our north, and the southern split will be \nabandoned over the southwest to eventually absorb abundant tropical \nmoisture associated with the remnants of Hurricane Polo. While \nprecipitable water will be well above average Monday through early \nWednesday, pushing around the 90th to 99th percentile of \nclimatology, forcing for ascent looks weak at best, and instability \nremains unimpressive. The official forecast maintains modest PoPs \n(mostly 20 to 50%) through Monday and into Tuesday morning, as we \ncan expect to see at least a few showers and storms around, but odds \nof significant rainfall are slim. Probabilities for total \nprecipitation (through Tuesday) over 0.1\" are lowest between I-25 \nand the WY/NE state-line, running around 20 to 50% here. Western \nNebraska and the higher terrain of southern Wyoming both have higher \nodds, between about 50 and 80%. That being said, expect rainfall to \nbe spotty, though the abundant atmospheric moisture will support the \npotential for highly localized heavy rainfall. A cool front moving \nthrough the area will knock down temperatures for Monday, with \nTuesday looking like the coolest day. \n\nFor the middle of the week, the most likely scenario puts our area \njust outside of the region of precipitation associated with a soggy \nclosed upper level low to our south. Ensembles are still all over \nthe place in terms of the location of this low pressure system as it \nejects eastward into the plains Wednesday into Thursday, but only \nthe far northern end of the envelope of solutions (10 to 20% \nprobability) would bring any impact to our area. The vast majority \nof ensemble members keep all rainfall with this system to our south, \nbut it is still worth a mention as a shift north would lead to a \nvastly different outcome than currently shown in the official \nforecast for Wednesday and Thursday. Expect temperatures to warm up \nthrough the latter half of the week, with highs peaking above \nseasonal averages on Friday. \n\nAnother low-confidence storm system is showing up in a handful of \nensemble members around next weekend. This will be another Pacific \ntrough moving from the northwest. In about 20% of ensembles, this \nsystem is strong enough to knock temperatures enough to support a \nwidespread freeze Sunday morning along with a quick shot of rain and \nsnow showers to the area. However, the other ~80% have light \nshowers and a modest cool down, with overall minimal impacts.",
